ur opponent has checked through all rounds so far — very passive. They checked pre-flop, flop, and turn.\n\nBut their passivity could mean either weakness or a trap (slow-playing a strong hand like a flush or straight).\n\nGiven the spade-heavy board, if they have a spade, they may already have a flush. Or they could be drawing.\n\nBut you have nothing — just high cards, no pair, no draw to a strong hand. The only draw you have is gutshot straight draw (need a 9 to make 9-10-J-Q-K), which is only four outs (four 9s in deck). ~8% chance to hit on river.\n\nBut even if you hit, it's a medium straight — vulnerable to flushes.\n\nAlso, you’re behind in cumulative chips and need to preserve your stack.\n\nCurrent valid actions: Fold (-1), Check (0), Raise min 100, or All-in (-2).\n\nSince opponent checked, you can check behind and see the river for free.\n\nBut should you bet? Probably not — you have no showdown value, and betting won’t make stronger hands fold (they’ll call or raise), and weaker hands (if any) likely won’t call.\n\nIs there any hand worse than yours that might call a bet? Maybe a lower high card like A-x or K-x with a blank? But unlikely — most decent hands would have bet.\n\nBluffing here is low EV — you have little fold equity, and the board is dangerous.\n\nBest play: **Check behind**, minimize loss, see the river, and evaluate whether to call a small bet if you improve (e.g., pair your jack or hit a 9).\n\nBut raising doesn’t gain much — you’re representing what? Top pair? But no jack pairs the board. Doesn’t make sense.\n\nSo checking is safe and optimal.\n\nReason:  \nYou have no pair and only a gutshot straight draw with 4 outs. The board is coordinated with three spades, making flushes possible. Opponent has been passive, possibly indicating strength (slow-play) or weakness. Checking allows you to see the river for free and avoid putting more chips in with a weak hand. Raising lacks credibility and risks building a pot you’re likely to lose. Folding is unnecessary since you can check. Thus, checking is the lowest-risk, highest-EV action.\n\nAnswer: 0"}
